// A value `if` whose arms have DIFFERENT integer widths must not silently
// return the wrong arm.
//
// `if a > 0 { 11 } else { 7i16 }` in an `Int`-returning function compiles
// today, and `f(-3)` prints 11 — the THEN arm's value, on the path that took
// the ELSE arm. The identical program with a bare `7` prints 7. Nothing warns,
// nothing panics, the exit code is 0: a wrong ANSWER, which is the worst thing
// a compiler can produce and the reason this is not merely a diagnostics bug.
//
// A suffixed literal is exact-typed (design 53) — the width-adopting rule is
// for a BARE literal — and Saw has no implicit integer conversion (design 170
// made every narrowing explicit and every widening a written `as`). So two
// arms at two widths is a type disagreement and belongs in the same error as
// any other arm-type mismatch, naming both types. That is the expectation
// below. (If the ruling instead widens same-sign arms implicitly, this pin
// becomes EXPECT: success printing 11 and 7 — but it can never stay as it is.)
//
// The loud sibling is DF-192f: `n * 2i16` on an `Int n` is a codegen ICE
// rather than a wrong answer. One root — nothing checks that two integer
// operands agree on width — and two faces.
//
// Found by tools/sawfuzz.py (its literal-rewrite mutation suffixes a bare
// literal); the ICE it reported minimized into this silent version.
// XFAIL: DF-192g a value `if` with mismatched-width integer arms compiles and returns the wrong arm's value
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: Int16

func f(a: Int) -> Int {
    if a > 0 { 11 } else { 7i16 }
}

func main() {
    print(f(3))
    print(f(-3))
}
